# React Composition Patterns

When building or refactoring React components:

- **Avoid boolean prop proliferation** (`isThread`, `isEditing`, …). Use composition and explicit variant components instead.
- **Use compound components** with a shared context; subcomponents read state/actions from context, not props.
- **Define a generic context interface** (`state`, `actions`, `meta`) so different providers can inject state; UI only depends on the interface.
- **Lift state into provider components** so siblings (e.g. dialog actions) can access state/actions without prop drilling.
- **Prefer composing `children`** over render props for structure; use render props when parent must pass data to the child.
- **React 19**: use `ref` as a normal prop (no `forwardRef`); use `use(Context)` instead of `useContext(Context)`.

**Example – compound component usage:**

```tsx
<Composer.Provider state={state} actions={actions} meta={meta}>
  <Composer.Frame>
    <Composer.Header />
    <Composer.Input />
    <Composer.Footer>
      <Composer.Formatting />
      <Composer.Submit />
    </Composer.Footer>
  </Composer.Frame>
</Composer.Provider>
```

